Introduction to Linux Access Control 
In Lab 02 you met a message you could not get past. Trying to enter /root as an ordinary user 
produced a refusal, and that refusal was not a bug. It was the first real security control you have 
encountered in this course, and this experiment takes it apart. 
Linux decides who may read, change or run every single file on the system using three pieces of 
information: who you are, which groups you belong to, and a short string of permission bits attached 
to the file itself. That mechanism is called discretionary access control, and variations of it protect 
almost every server you will ever defend. 
This matters far beyond tidiness. A large share of real Linux compromises do not begin with a clever 
exploit. They begin with a file that anyone could write to, a script that runs with more authority than it 
needs, or an administrator who worked as root because it was quicker. By the end of this session 
you will be able to spot all three. 
Why Linux has users at all 
Linux inherited its design from systems that were shared by many people at once, where one 
person’s mistake had to be prevented from becoming everybody’s mistake. Even on a laptop used 
by one student, that design is what stops a program you downloaded from quietly reading every file 
on the disk. 
Think of a university hostel. Every student has a room and a key that opens only that room. There 
are shared spaces that a whole floor may use, a warden who can open any door, and rooms that 
nobody except the warden may enter. Nobody argues that this is unfriendly; it is simply how a 
shared building works safely. Linux accounts, groups and permissions are the same three ideas.

