Accounts are listed in /etc/passwd, a plain text file readable by everyone. Despite the name it has 
held no passwords for decades. One line of /etc/passwd, taken apart: 
kali:x:1000:1000:Kali User:/home/kali:/usr/bin/zsh 
  |      | |    |     |         |         | 
  |      | |    |     |         |         +-- login shell 
  |      | |    |     |         +------------ home directory 
  |      | |    |     +---------------------- description field 
  |      | |    +---------------------------- primary group ID (GID) 
  |      | +--------------------------------- user ID (UID) 
        | +----------------------------------- password is elsewhere 
          +-------------------------------------- username 
The real password hashes live in /etc/shadow, which only root can read. Splitting the two files was 
itself a security fix: programs need to look up usernames constantly, but nothing ordinary needs to 
see the hashes. 
Notice the last field. An account whose shell is /usr/sbin/nologin or /bin/false cannot be logged in to 
at all. When you audit a machine, a service account whose shell has quietly been changed to a real 
one is a finding worth reporting. 
