Groups 
A group is a named set of users, so that permission can be granted to several people at once 
instead of one at a time. In the hostel analogy, a group is the set of keys that open the second-floor 
common room. 
Every user has exactly one primary group, recorded in /etc/passwd, which becomes the group 
owner of files they create. A user may also belong to any number of supplementary groups, listed 
in /etc/group. On Kali, membership of the sudo group is what allows an ordinary account to act as 
administrator. 
