===== PAGE 9 =====

The first column of ls -l is ten characters. The first says what kind of thing it is; the remaining nine are 
three groups of three. 
-rwxr-xr-- 1 kali students 1247 Sep 24 10:14 report.txt 
|  |  | 
| |  |  +-- others: read only 
| |  +----- group: read and execute 
| +-------- owner: read, write and execute 
+---------- type: - file, d directory, l symbolic link 
Each triad is always printed in the same order, r then w then x, with a dash where the permission is 
absent. The trap for beginners is that the three letters mean different things depending on whether 
the target is a file or a directory. 
Bit 
On a file 
On a directory 
r (read) 
You can look at the contents. 
You can list the names inside it. 
w (write) 
You can change or overwrite it. 
You can create, rename and delete 
entries inside it. 
x (execute) 
You can run it as a program. 
You can enter it and reach things inside. 
 
Two consequences catch people out every semester. A directory with r but no x lets you see the 
names inside and nothing else. More seriously, deleting a file depends on write permission on the 
directory, not on the file: if you can write to a folder you can delete a read-only file inside it. 
Numeric (Octal) Notation 
Each triad is three bits, so it can be written as a single digit from 0 to 7. Read means 4, write means 
2, execute means 1, and you add them together. 
