Changing Permissions and Ownership 
chmod changes the permission bits and accepts either notation. The symbolic form is easier to read 
when you are adjusting one thing; the numeric form is faster when you are setting all nine bits at 
once. 
chmod u+x script.sh      # give the owner execute 
chmod g-w report.txt     # take write away from the group 
chmod o= secrets.txt     # give others nothing 
chmod a+r notes.txt      # everyone may read 
chmod 640 report.txt     # owner rw, group r, others nothing 
chmod -R 750 project/    # apply down a whole directory tree 
chown changes the user owner and chgrp the group owner. Both normally require root, because 
otherwise you could dispose of an incriminating file by giving it to somebody else. 
sudo chown ahmed report.txt            # change the user owner 
sudo chown ahmed:students report.txt    # change both at once 
sudo chgrp students report.txt          # change only the group 
