The clearest example of SUID is /usr/bin/passwd. Changing your own password means writing to 
/etc/shadow, which only root may do, yet any user can change their password. The program carries 
the SUID bit and therefore runs as root for that one job. 
The clearest example of the sticky bit is /tmp. Everybody can write there, which would normally mean 
everybody can delete everybody else’s files. The sticky bit prevents exactly that. 
