Conclusion 
In this experiment you moved from being a user of a Linux machine to being able to control who else 
may use it. You examined how accounts and groups are stored, created both, and saw why a 
service account is deliberately built so that nobody can log in to it. 
You then read the permission string character by character, changed it in both symbolic and numeric 
form, and proved each change by switching to another account and being refused. You saw that 
read, write and execute mean different things on a directory, that the right to delete a file belongs to 
whoever can write to the folder holding it, and that default permissions come from the umask rather 
than from nowhere.
